{"qid":"Q39","names":["Swiss Confederation"],"title":"Swiss Confederation","endonym":{"native":"Schweizerische Eidgenossenschaft","lang":"de"},"capsule":"The Swiss Confederation (Schweizerische Eidgenossenschaft) traces its formal origin to the Federal Charter of 1291 among Uri, Schwyz, and Unterwalden, though the Confederation's continuous political existence as a recognized entity grew through fourteenth-century military victories over the Habsburgs, including Morgarten (1315) and Sempach (1386). By the late fifteenth century it comprised thirteen cantons and had achieved de facto independence from the Holy Roman Empire, formalized in the Peace of Westphalia in 1648. The Confederation was briefly dissolved by Napoleon's Helvetic Republic (1798–1803) before restoration; the 1848 federal constitution transformed the loose alliance into a modern federal state with a bicameral legislature, which—with the 1874 constitutional revision introducing the referendum—forms the basis of the present Swiss polity.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Switzerland's modern boundaries are precisely defined by international treaty; the medieval Confederation's territorial extent expanded incrementally and is well-documented in cantonal charters, though early boundaries with neighboring lordships were fluid.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Switzerland","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Switzerland"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Federal Charter of 1291","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Federal_Charter_of_1291"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Old Swiss Confederacy","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Old_Swiss_Confederacy